  "body": "All of the browsers implement it.\r\n```idl\r\ninterface HTMLStyleElement : HTMLElement {\r\n  attribute boolean disabled\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne difference that I found - the behavior differs between Internet Explorer 11 and the rest of the gang (I tried Chrome 54 and Firefox 50).\r\n```js\r\nvar css = 'body { transform: rotate(10deg) }';\r\nvar style = document.createElement('style');\r\nstyle.innerHTML = css;\r\nstyle.disabled = true;\r\ndocument.head.appendChild(style);\r\n```\r\nIn Internet Explorer, the page will not rotate. In the rest of the gang, the page will rotate.\r\nSetting `disabled` to `true` after the element is appended to the DOM -\r\n```js\r\nvar css = 'body { transform: rotate(10deg) }';\r\nvar style = document.createElement('style');\r\nstyle.innerHTML = css;\r\nstyle.disabled = true;\r\ndocument.head.appendChild(style);\r\n```\r\nShows the same results (does not rotate) for all of the tested browsers.\r\n\r\nI think that the Internet Explorer 11 way of doing things in this case is more predictable, but backwards compatibility might prevent standardizing its behavior (I hope not :().\r\nHowever, I checked the same situation with `<link>` and it seems to behave the same as `<style>` in Internet Explorer 11 (does not rotate) in all of the tested browsers except Firefox 50 (rotates).\r\n```js\r\nvar css = 'body { transform: rotate(10deg) }';\r\nvar style = document.createElement('link');\r\nstyle.rel = 'stylesheet';\r\nstyle.href = \"data:text/css,\" + css;\r\nstyle.disabled = true;\r\ndocument.head.appendChild(style);\r\n```",
